d check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Be is needed before the -ing form.","B":"After will use base be, not been.","C":"This is a passive-shaped phrase, not the requested active continuous.","D":"Future continuous pictures an activity at that moment: **will be holding**."}}},{"id":"e86a6eea-29f1-58ab-ae2c-05553bf9b676","topicSlug":"future-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Use future perfect simple: By Friday, they ____ the door open.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"will has held"},{"id":"B","text":"will have holding"},{"id":"C","text":"will had held"},{"id":"D","text":"will have held"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"By gives a future deadline. Build **will + have + participle**.","explanation":"The future perfect simple is **will have held**, looking back from Friday.","keywords":["future-completion"],"skill":"future-completion","contextKey":"future-perfect-continuous-48","fingerprint":"14756596b6b464f32e23b527cfcafc7018ea7771f64fc3174cfccd6f37c41beb","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["By gives a future deadline. Build **will + have + participle**.","The future perfect simple is **will have held**, looking back from Friday.","Choose **will have held**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Have must remain base form after will.","B":"Perfect simple needs a past participle.","C":"Had cannot follow will directly.","D":"The future perfect simple is **will have held**, looking back from Friday."}}},{"id":"d97b71aa-4663-564b-a20c-49a2c23d539f","topicSlug":"future-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Use future perfect continuous: By noon, I ____ the door open for two hours.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"will been holding"},{"id":"B","text":"will have be holding"},{"id":"C","text":"will have been holding"},{"id":"D","text":"will have being holding"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Combine the future auxiliary with **have been + -ing** to measure duration.","explanation":"The duration measured at noon is expressed by **will have been holding**.","keywords":["future-duration"],"skill":"future-duration","contextKey":"future-perfect-continuous-48","fingerprint":"06b548be8728f9cfabb69c4bf662bd280a05482a1a9536c098c52c85b30bf814","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Combine the future auxiliary with **have been + -ing** to measure duration.","The duration measured at noon is expressed by **will have been holding**.","Choose **will have been holding**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Have is missing and will cannot take been directly.","B":"Have needs been, not base be.","C":"The duration measured at noon is expressed by **will have been holding**.","D":"Use the past participle been, not being, after have."}}},{"id":"9182d79c-b68c-5fa2-a604-5186c497ffc2","topicSlug":"future-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Choose the opening for a future-perfect question about the door open: ____ they have held it by Friday?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Have"},{"id":"B","text":"Do"},{"id":"C","text":"Will have"},{"id":"D","text":"Will"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"In a multi-auxiliary question, move **only the first auxiliary**.","explanation":"Only **will** moves before the subject; have stays after they.","keywords":["future-question-order"],"skill":"future-question-order","contextKey":"future-perfect-continuous-48","fingerprint":"e9e4599f5dd0c6c247cbca00b40589be298dccd547b80c099c816cf2461c2964","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["In a multi-auxiliary question, move **only the first auxiliary**.","Only **will** moves before the subject; have stays after they.","Choose **Will**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The sentence already has have after they; the future auxiliary is missing.","B":"Do is not added when will is the first auxiliary.","C":"This would repeat have and put two auxiliaries before the subject.","D":"Only **will** moves before the subject; have stays after they."}}},{"id":"b5d1e717-6742-5491-a8c3-8a7347f31ac5","topicSlug":"future-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Use future continuous: At ten tomorrow, we ____ the meeting records.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"will be kept"},{"id":"B","text":"will be keeping"},{"id":"C","text":"will keeping"},{"id":"D","text":"will been keeping"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Picture the activity **in progress at a future time**.","explanation":"Future continuous pictures an activity at that moment: **will be keeping**.","keywords":["future-in-progress"],"skill":"future-in-progress","contextKey":"future-perfect-continuous-49","fingerprint":"8a1511ebb4b2c7fd7b1c10b62169a952e29f285ded5485a4de359c7244ab071b","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Picture the activity **in progress at a future time**.","Future continuous pictures an activity at that moment: **will be keeping**.","Choose **will be keeping**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This is a passive-shaped phrase, not the requested active continuous.","B":"Future continuous pictures an activity at that moment: **will be keeping**.","C":"Be is needed before the -ing form.","D":"After will use base be, not been."}}},{"id":"5f1e8ac4-aeff-50b6-ac51-e7d52edf6562","topicSlug":"future-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Use future perfect simple: By Friday, they ____ the meeting records.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"will has kept"},{"id":"B","text":"will have keeping"},{"id":"C","text":"will had kept"},{"id":"D","text":"will have kept"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"By gives a future deadline. Build **will + have + participle**.","explanation":"The future perfect simple is **will have kept**, looking back from Friday.","keywords":["future-completion"],"skill":"future-completion","contextKey":"future-perfect-continuous-49","fingerprint":"c70837b6504dc452e13631cdc4de0b4df3ae449e20dec2a6d737fcf731b35ba5","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["By gives a future deadline. Build **will + have + participle**.","The future perfect simple is **will have kept**, looking back from Friday.","Choose **will have kept**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Have must remain base form after will.","B":"Perfect simple needs a past participle.","C":"Had cannot follow will directly.","D":"The future perfect simple is **will have kept**, looking back from Friday."}}},{"id":"b804b555-98f9-5d82-aa7c-d6f95d98bdf2","topicSlug":"future-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Use future perfect continuous: By noon, I ____ the meeting records for two hours.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"will have being keeping"},{"id":"B","text":"will been keeping"},{"id":"C","text":"will have be keeping"},{"id":"D","text":"will have been keeping"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Combine the future auxiliary with **have been + -ing** to measure duration.","explanation":"The duration measured at noon is expressed by **will have been keeping**.","keywords":["future-duration"],"skill":"future-duration","contextKey":"future-perfect-continuous-49","fingerprint":"7b8f940292625db83183b8a2103cee6db7a8115b753996a9fa38ee784acbe5cc","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Combine the future auxiliary with **have been + -ing** to measure duration.","The duration measured at noon is expressed by **will have been keeping**.","Choose **will have been keeping**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Use the past participle been, not being, after have.","B":"Have is missing and will cannot take been directly.","C":"Have needs been, not base be.","D":"The duration measured at noon is expressed by **will have been keeping**."}}},{"id":"7729b2ba-4b0f-5f74-a188-d45b3d0cef6e","topicSlug":"future-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Choose the opening for a future-perfect question about the meeting records: ____ they have kept it by Friday?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Will"},{"id":"B","text":"Have"},{"id":"C","text":"Do"},{"id":"D","text":"Will have"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"In a multi-auxiliary question, move **only the first auxiliary**.","explanation":"Only **will** moves before the subject; have stays after they.","keywords":["future-question-order"],"skill":"future-question-order","contextKey":"future-perfect-continuous-49","fingerprint":"e0ffdf1317f6973e46dc3ec6bd73c3fd2ccc08fdf88e481ba2de36ac44ac2ecc","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["In a multi-auxiliary question, move **only the first auxiliary**.","Only **will** moves before the subject; have stays after they.","Choose **Will**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Only **will** moves before the subject; have stays after they.","B":"The sentence already has have after they; the future auxiliary is missing.","C":"Do is not added when will is the first auxiliary.","D":"This would repeat have and put two auxiliaries before the subject."}}},{"id":"bb5edc57-7eef-5d5e-aeb0-bc4e189bba56","topicSlug":"future-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Use future continuous: At ten tomorrow, we ____ the kitchen floor.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"will be swept"},{"id":"B","text":"will be sweeping"},{"id":"C","text":"will sweeping"},{"id":"D","text":"will been sweeping"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Picture the activity **in progress at a future time**.","explanation":"Future continuous pictures an activity at that moment: **will be sweeping**.","keywords":["future-in-progress"],"skill":"future-in-progress","contextKey":"future-perfect-continuous-50","fingerprint":"e6248323eb31391170e78c458c7a2f16fa5357517f79babf1ec158e029c21611","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Picture the activity **in progress at a future time**.","Future continuous pictures an activity at that moment: **will be sweeping**.","Choose **will be sweeping**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This is a passive-shaped phrase, not the requested active continuous.","B":"Future continuous pictures an activity at that moment: **will be sweeping**.","C":"Be is needed before the -ing form.","D":"After will use base be, not been."}}},{"id":"4f781aea-4ea2-5cf1-afba-56c16adaf6eb","topicSlug":"future-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Use future perfect simple: By Friday, they ____ the kitchen floor.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"will has swept"},{"id":"B","text":"will have sweeping"},{"id":"C","text":"will had swept"},{"id":"D","text":"will have swept"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"By gives a future deadline. Build **will + have + participle**.","explanation":"The future perfect simple is **will have swept**, looking back from Friday.","keywords":["future-completion"],"skill":"future-completion","contextKey":"future-perfect-continuous-50","fingerprint":"19c6826ccfc1c5d41877456b897000adf276428340d7b39a2d763336058fe229","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["By gives a future deadline. Build **will + have + participle**.","The future perfect simple is **will have swept**, looking back from Friday.","Choose **will have swept**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Have must remain base form after will.","B":"Perfect simple needs a past participle.","C":"Had cannot follow will directly.","D":"The future perfect simple is **will have swept**, looking back from Friday."}}},{"id":"c1fe2f61-1a5c-5e93-a142-4207f928300a","topicSlug":"future-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Use future perfect continuous: By noon, I ____ the kitchen floor for two hours.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"will have been sweeping"},{"id":"B","text":"will have being sweeping"},{"id":"C","text":"will been sweeping"},{"id":"D","text":"will have be sweeping"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Combine the future auxiliary with **have been + -ing** to measure duration.","explanation":"The duration measured at noon is expressed by **will have been sweeping**.","keywords":["future-duration"],"skill":"future-duration","contextKey":"future-perfect-continuous-50","fingerprint":"29776af8114473f6f70f224d802a8fbab567985993cd0c679f0607a5b283e3b6","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Combine the future auxiliary with **have been + -ing** to measure duration.","The duration measured at noon is expressed by **will have been sweeping**.","Choose **will have been sweeping**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The duration measured at noon is expressed by **will have been sweeping**.","B":"Use the past participle been, not being, after have.","C":"Have is missing and will cannot take been directly.","D":"Have needs been, not base be."}}},{"id":"d309b7ce-2334-503c-a2f6-578a9ef2aa37","topicSlug":"future-perfect-continuous","level":"B2","prompt":"Choose the opening for a future-perfect question about the kitchen floor: ____ they have swept it by Friday?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Will"},{"id":"B","text":"Have"},{"id":"C","text":"Do"},{"id":"D","text":"Will have"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"In a multi-auxiliary question, move **only the first auxiliary**.","explanation":"Only **will** moves before the subject; have stays after they.","keywords":["future-question-order"],"skill":"future-question-order","contextKey":"future-perfect-continuous-50","fingerprint":"71ca741936cb396eec5e2a75ccbb647cfcd7972aec9848b0d55e467bf62ec84b","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["In a multi-auxiliary question, move **only the first auxiliary**.","Only **will** moves before the subject; have stays after they.","Choose **Will**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Only **will** moves before the subject; have stays after they.","B":"The sentence already has have after they; the future auxiliary is missing.","C":"Do is not added when will is the first auxiliary.","D":"This would repeat have and put two auxiliaries before the subject."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Look ahead to an activity in progress, a completed result or a duration measured from a future point.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"An activity at a future moment","explanation":"Use **will be + -ing** to picture an activity already in progress at a future moment. You are looking into the middle of it. This differs from will + base verb, which can simply predict an event or express a decision.","pattern":"At noon, we **will be travelling**.","commonMistakes":["A precise future time does not always require continuous; the intended viewpoint decides."],"examples":["Use future continuous: At ten tomorrow, we **will be preparing** the visitor guide.","Use future continuous: At ten tomorrow, we **will be restoring** the old portrait."],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Completion by a future deadline","explanation":"Use **will have + past participle** to look back from a future point at something expected to be complete. By means no later than that point. Until generally describes continuation up to a point, so it is not interchangeable with by.","pattern":"By noon, we **will have arrived**.","commonMistakes":["Will have arrived describes completion by a deadline; will arrive until noon does not express that meaning."],"examples":["Use future perfect simple: By Friday, they **will have prepared** the visitor guide.","Use future perfect simple: By Friday, they **will have restored** the old portrait."],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Duration up to a future point","explanation":"Use **will have been + -ing** for the duration of an activity measured at a future point. It combines future reference, perfect aspect and continuous aspect. The first auxiliary is will; the later forms stay have and been.","pattern":"By July, I **will have been working** here for a year.","commonMistakes":["State verbs usually favour future perfect simple: will have known, rather than will have been knowing."],"examples":["Use future perfect continuous: By noon, I **will have been preparing** the visitor guide for two hours.","Use future perfect continuous: By noon, I **will have been restoring** the old portrait for two hours."],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Questions and negatives","explanation":"Move **will** before the subject in a question. Put **not** after will in a negative. All later auxiliaries stay after the subject in their original order. In conversation, will not often becomes won’t.","pattern":"Will she **have finished**? / She **will not have finished**.","commonMistakes":["Do not change have to has after will, even with she, he or it."],"examples":["Choose the opening for a future-perfect question about the visitor guide: **Will** they have prepared it by Friday?","Choose the opening for a future-perfect question about the old portrait: **Will** they have restored it by Friday?"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["At noon, we **will be travelling**.","By noon, we **will have arrived**.","By July, I **will have been working** here for a year.","Will she **have finished**? / She **will not have finished**."]},"stage":"3 · Time and experience","prerequisites":["past-perfect-continuous"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
